--- body ---
## Context

The raid on tickets 0550-0555 (conductor implementation) found that SPEC.md
§5.2.2's content schema — `entries`, `slabs`, `passages`, the per-field FTS5
table — has zero footprint anywhere in the fork, and no open ticket owns
building it as code: 0028 builds the seg/1 segmenter *algorithm* (logical,
works on flat text, not SQL); 0034 is an upstream *issue draft*, explicitly
"no code to accept". Tranches 4 and 5 (0554, 0555) dispatch against this
schema and cannot proceed without it.

Tranches 1-2 (0551, 0552, both merged) already built the schema's
*bookkeeping* half in the fork's `ledger.ts`: `origins`, `libraries`,
`cursors`, `item_census`, `fulltext_census`, `stage_queue`, `leases`, each
under one `ledger_meta`/`schemaVersion` stamp. That file's own docstring
already names it "search-index-v2's bookkeeping half" — this ticket is the
other half, in the same file and the same schema, per §5.2.2's own framing
("one file, one schema, every row library-keyed"). Do not create a
competing schema or a second stamp.

Author directive, 2026-09-01: the schema lands in `search-index-v2.sqlite`,
a real file that exists in **parallel** with v1's `search-index.sqlite`, so
v1 development (the in-flight stopword series, PRs #4/#5/#6 on the fork)
continues undisturbed. This ticket touches nothing under
`src/features/search/` outside the `conductor/` module.

On closer reading, tranche 3 (0553, extract shim) is **not** blocked by
this gap: its actions — the fetch, class-order dispatch, the
first-with-text choice function — run entirely against tables tranche 1
already built (`stage_queue`'s `class_rank`, `item_census`,
`fulltext_census`). Only tranche 4's chunk worker ("chunk worker over
stored text") and tranche 5's band dispatch (K = passages per item) need
`entries`/`slabs`/`passages` to exist. If the author resumes tranche 3
separately, it does not need to wait on this ticket.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.2 (Storage: one file, one schema, every row library-keyed)
  — authoritative: WAL mode, `synchronous=NORMAL`, `busy_timeout=5000` on
  every connection, `PRAGMA auto_vacuum=INCREMENTAL` set **before the first
  table** (a no-op if set later — the section says so explicitly); the
  entry layer (`entries`, `slabs`, `passages` columns and shapes); FTS5
  per-field columns and tokenizer (`unicode61 remove_diacritics 2`),
  contentless (`content=''`, `contentless_delete=1`) where SQLite >= 3.43,
  v1's external-content layout as the probed fallback recorded in meta.
- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/ledger.ts` — the bookkeeping half
  already built (tranches 1-2): `Ledger.open(path)` is file-path
  parameterized (no production call site yet — only test fixtures use it
  today), `createSchema()` is one `db.exec` DDL block under a single
  `ledger_meta`/`schemaVersion` stamp. Extend this, don't compete with it.
- Fork `src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts` — v1's schema-stamp discipline
  to mirror as a *pattern* (stamp read before any DDL/write; a database
  this build does not understand is moved aside, never deleted or
  overwritten) — read for the shape, do not import from or modify it; v1
  stays untouched, byte-for-byte.
- Fork `src/features/search/index-manager.ts`, `backend.ts` — v1's backend
  factory/interface shape, so a future v2 backend has somewhere sane to
  plug in later. Wiring an actual v2 backend into the query path is out of
  scope here — just don't collide with the existing module layout.

## Actions

1. Extend `ledger.ts`'s `createSchema()` (same file, same `db.exec` block,
   same connection — or a sibling module writing through the same open
   `Database` handle) with §5.2.2's content tables: `entries`, `slabs`,
   `passages`, and the FTS5 table, per the spec's exact column lists and
   constraints.
2. Wire a real production file path: `search-index-v2.sqlite`, opened with
   every pragma §5.2.2 specifies, in the order it specifies (auto_vacuum
   before the first table — this is the one ordering trap the spec calls
   out by name).
3. Schema-stamp discipline: the content tables bump the *same*
   `ledger_meta`/`schemaVersion` the bookkeeping half already stamps — one
   file, one schema, one version number, per §5.2.2's own framing. Don't
   invent a second stamp for "the other half" of the same file.
4. Purely additive: no file under `src/features/search/` outside
   `conductor/` changes. `search-index.sqlite` and every v1 module are
   untouched.
5. Explicitly out of scope: the calibration header (SPEC.md marks it "owed
   here, and not by this entry" — a later ticket); the segmenter itself
   (0028); actually writing rows from a running pipeline (tranches 4/5).
   This ticket builds the schema and direct read/write primitives for it
   (insert/query helpers, the same shape `ledger.ts` already provides for
   its own tables) — not the segmentation logic that will call them.

## Test

- Red first: assert every §5.2.2 table, column, and CHECK constraint is
  present, on a fresh file, before any pipeline code depends on them.
- The ordering trap named in Actions 2: a test that sets `auto_vacuum`
  *after* the first table exists and asserts it did NOT take effect (the
  spec's own documented no-op), beside one that sets it first and asserts
  it did — so a future edit that reorders the pragmas is caught red rather
  than silently losing incremental vacuum.
- Coexistence: open `search-index.sqlite` (v1) and `search-index-v2.sqlite`
  in the same data directory concurrently, write to both, confirm no lock
  contention beyond ordinary per-file WAL semantics and no cross-file
  interference.

## Verification

- [ ] Every §5.2.2 table/column/CHECK present and tested
- [ ] `auto_vacuum=INCREMENTAL` ordering trap has a red-first test on both
      sides (set-before-first-table works; set-after is provably a no-op)
- [ ] v1's own existing test suite, run unmodified, stays green — proof
      `search-index.sqlite` and its code path are untouched, not just an
      unchanged-diff claim
- [ ] Both files coexist under concurrent open in one data directory
- [ ] Positive control per fixture/assertion (house discipline): each new
      test demonstrated failing against a deliberately broken schema
      before trusted

## Invariants

- `search-index.sqlite`, `sqlite-index.ts`, and every file under
  `src/features/search/` outside `conductor/` are untouched.
- Upstream tests green throughout.
- One file, one schema, one stamp: the bookkeeping half (tranches 1-2) and
  the content half (this ticket) share `search-index-v2.sqlite` and its
  single `schemaVersion`.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] entries/slabs/passages/FTS schema merged on a fork branch, built on
      tranche 2's tip (`tranche-2-conductor-election` @ `a00091b`, or a
      later tranche's tip if more have landed by the time this starts)
- [ ] `search-index-v2.sqlite` exists as a real, opened, production file
      path — not only exercised from test fixtures
- [ ] Ticket 0554 (tranche 4: chunk/embed workers) has its `Blocked-by`
      updated to include this ticket
